WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.700
If warming quiets the cool line, what happens to warmth when that line is gone?

2
00:00:08.733 --> 00:00:14.573
First, the task. A head-fixed mouse rests a forepaw on a small plate at 32 degrees.

3
00:00:15.533 --> 00:00:16.773
It licks to report a change.

4
00:00:17.767 --> 00:00:20.667
Trained mice reported a warming step of 1 degree.

5
00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:26.080
A mouse that licked on every trial would score hits without detecting anything.

6
00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:30.593
So some trials are catch trials, with no temperature change.

7
00:00:31.453 --> 00:00:32.793
A lick then is a false alarm.

8
00:00:33.783 --> 00:00:36.183
An index called d′ compares the two.

9
00:00:37.023 --> 00:00:39.883
It separates detection from how freely the mouse licks.

10
00:00:40.833 --> 00:00:45.833
Licking as often on catch trials as on warming trials gives a d′ prime near zero.

11
00:00:49.433 --> 00:00:50.713
Now the knockouts.

12
00:00:50.713 --> 00:00:56.173
TRPM8 is the main channel behind the cool fibres' response to cooling.

13
00:00:57.183 --> 00:01:01.263
Remove it. What happens to the cool fibres' resting firing?

14
00:01:02.263 --> 00:01:03.743
And can the mouse still report warming?

15
00:01:09.833 --> 00:01:14.133
Without TRPM8, the cool fibres lose their ongoing firing.

16
00:01:15.113 --> 00:01:16.773
Warming has nothing left to silence.

17
00:01:17.817 --> 00:01:22.457
And the mice failed completely to report warming, even after 10 days of training.

18
00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:28.990
Not because heat opens TRPM8.

19
00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:33.410
Removing it removes the firing whose fall is part of the warmth signal.

20
00:01:34.383 --> 00:01:36.243
Now remove heat channels instead.

21
00:01:37.243 --> 00:01:42.223
Mice without TRPV1, or without TRPM2, still detected warming.

22
00:01:43.333 --> 00:01:47.853
Even mice lacking three heat channels still detected warming, with reduced

23
00:01:47.853 --> 00:01:50.713
sensitivity. Heat-channel loss blunts.

24
00:01:51.853 --> 00:01:55.193
TRPM8 loss abolished the cold report in this task.

25
00:01:56.117 --> 00:02:01.557
And TRPM8 knockout mice still had warm-excited fibres, in normal proportions.

26
00:02:02.667 --> 00:02:07.327
If each line alone were enough to drive a report, the remaining warm responses

27
00:02:07.327 --> 00:02:08.507
should have supported one.

28
00:02:09.387 --> 00:02:10.727
In these mice, they did not.

29
00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:16.160
The findings support a role for reduced cool input.

30
00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:22.200
Warm-excited input may also contribute, but its separate effect on sensitivity was

31
00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:23.200
not isolated.

32
00:02:24.083 --> 00:02:26.703
How the brain combines the two lines is not known.

33
00:02:30.217 --> 00:02:36.397
One gap remains. The resting cool firing was recorded in isolated skin, not in a

34
00:02:36.397 --> 00:02:37.397
behaving mouse.

35
00:02:38.067 --> 00:02:41.667
The skin under the stimulator sat at the same 32 degrees.

36
00:02:42.727 --> 00:02:45.147
Joining the two is still an inference, not a recording.

37
00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:48.790
Another preparation points the other way.

38
00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:55.010
In mouth-warming experiments, blocking TRPV1 removed the imaged warmth responses.

39
00:02:56.183 --> 00:03:00.923
So does gentle warmth rest mainly on warm fibres, or on quieter cool fibres?

40
00:03:01.723 --> 00:03:02.743
That is not settled.

41
00:03:03.783 --> 00:03:05.163
Keep the bath in mind.

42
00:03:05.163 --> 00:03:09.643
A resting condition is part of the stimulus, not a neutral background.
